  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday morning through next Saturday)\nIssued at 204 PM EST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nThe long term forecast sees little in the way of change with this\nupdate. The beginning of the week through Wednesday still looks \nto be warm as the upper level jet remains to the north of the area\nand our current air mass warms a bit thanks to copious sunshine. \nHigh temperatures Tuesday and Wednesday will be 5 to 10 degrees F \nabove average, which corresponds to temperatures in the 70s for \nmost (a few 60s in far north Georgia, especially at elevation).\n\nUncertainty remains going into Thursday regarding our \nprecipitation chances. The ensemble trend for this forecast period\nwas to pull the main surface pressure falls further to the north \nacross the average. Intensification of the jet streak within the \nbroad trough forecast to dig into the eastern CONUS doesn't happen\nin the mean until after the axis pushes in overhead. K-mean \nclustering alongside some analysis of individual members shows \nshort waves progressing through the western side of the trough in \na more unbiased manner, then phasing with the larger trough going \ninto late Thursday night and Friday morning. The overall net \neffect is to push any PoPs mostly across northern Georgia on \nThursday afternoon and evening that would be associated with the \npush of a front into the area. Given the complex nature of the \nupper level flow details, I'd expect PoPs to continue to adjust a \nbit run to run over the next few days.\n\nCold front pushes through the area into Friday. Forecast highs on\nFriday are 5-10 degrees below average now, as it does look like \nthe front will progress through most all of north and central \nGeorgia by the afternoon. That brings us on of the cooler days of \nthe year so far, only getting into the 50s and low 60s (and some \nhigh 40s across far north Georgia). A hard freeze is likely coming\nfor portions of north Georgia Friday night into Saturday morning,\nand again on Saturday night into Sunday morning. Lows in north \nGeorgia should be in the upper 20s, with low 30s into the metro \nand Athens and mid 30s into central Georgia.\n\nOne additional thing that may need to start being mentioned - the\npotential for some mountain precip early Friday morning in NE \nGeorgia. Current forecast temps aren't quite cold enough for any \nsnow mention - still a bit early in the season. But worth keeping \nan eye on in the highest elevations, as a small swing in the \nforecast could bring some early season chances in. Would put the \noverall probability very low at this time (under 10%).",
